WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:05.244
This is an architectural overview of the TCP/IP protocol suite.

2
00:00:05.594 --> 00:00:12.506
Where it came from, how its four layers fit together, and what each core protocol does as data travels from one host to another.

3
00:00:14.633 --> 00:00:25.236
The Transmission Control Protocol and Internet Protocol, TCP/IP, is the suite of protocols that lets computers communicate across wide area networks and the Internet.

4
00:00:25.586 --> 00:00:35.741
Its development began in the late nineteen sixties and early seventies, funded by the United States Department of Defense, through its Advanced Research Projects Agency, DARPA.

5
00:00:36.091 --> 00:00:43.045
DARPA experimented with packet switching: data is broken into blocks, and each block finds its own way across the network.

6
00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:46.895
The milestones came quickly.

7
00:00:47.245 --> 00:00:52.429
In nineteen seventy, ARPANET hosts adopted the Network Control Protocol, N C P.

8
00:00:52.779 --> 00:01:00.544
In nineteen seventy two, the first Telnet specification was published as RFC 318, for remote terminal access.

9
00:01:00.894 --> 00:01:06.825
In nineteen seventy three, the File Transfer Protocol followed, in RFC 454.

10
00:01:07.175 --> 00:01:22.897
In nineteen seventy four, RFC 675 specified TCP, the protocol at the heart of TCP/IP. Its first name was the Transmission Control Program; it became the Transmission Control Protocol when IP was split out of it.

11
00:01:23.247 --> 00:01:30.437
In nineteen eighty one, RFC 791 defined the Internet Protocol, its addressing and its routing.

12
00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:38.829
In nineteen eighty two, the Defense Communications Agency and ARPA adopted TCP and IP as the standard for military networks.

13
00:01:39.179 --> 00:01:43.873
In nineteen eighty three, ARPANET switched from N C P to TCP/IP.

14
00:01:44.223 --> 00:01:49.492
And in nineteen eighty four, the Domain Name System arrived, so that hosts could be found by name.

15
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:59.749
Requests for Comments from the early nineteen eighties show that when TCP/IP was standardized, it was a Department of Defense standard.

16
00:02:00.099 --> 00:02:05.198
RFC 760 is titled: DoD Standard Internet Protocol.

17
00:02:05.548 --> 00:02:10.583
RFC 761: DoD Standard Transmission Control Protocol.

18
00:02:10.933 --> 00:02:19.679
And RFC 810: DoD Internet Host Table Specification. Before DNS, every host kept a table of names and addresses.

19
00:02:20.029 --> 00:02:32.531
RFC 791 and RFC 793 later replaced 760 and 761, and they remain the foundation of IP and TCP today.

20
00:02:34.633 --> 00:02:41.439
The Internet Protocol is designed for use in interconnected systems of packet-switched computer communication networks.

21
00:02:41.789 --> 00:02:46.333
It transmits blocks of data, called datagrams, from sources to destinations.

22
00:02:46.683 --> 00:02:51.312
Sources and destinations are hosts, identified by fixed-length addresses.

23
00:02:51.662 --> 00:02:58.062
And IP provides for fragmentation and reassembly of long datagrams, when they must pass through small packet networks.

24
00:03:00.167 --> 00:03:03.260
TCP/IP is organized in four layers.

25
00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.959
At the bottom, the network interface layer puts frames on the wire.

26
00:03:07.309 --> 00:03:13.048
Above it, the internet layer moves datagrams between networks, with IP, ICMP and ARP.

27
00:03:13.398 --> 00:03:18.177
The transport layer gives applications a conversation, with TCP and UDP.

28
00:03:18.527 --> 00:03:27.273
And at the top, the application layer is where protocols such as HTTP, DNS, FTP and Telnet live, reached through the sockets interface.

29
00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.197
As data moves down the stack, each layer wraps it in its own header.

30
00:03:33.547 --> 00:03:38.347
TCP adds a header with ports and sequence numbers, and the result is a segment.

31
00:03:38.697 --> 00:03:43.092
IP adds a header with the source and destination addresses: a datagram.

32
00:03:43.442 --> 00:03:48.391
The network interface adds a frame header and a trailer, with hardware addresses and a checksum.

33
00:03:48.741 --> 00:03:54.203
At the receiving host the process runs in reverse, each layer removing the header its peer added.

34
00:03:56.333 --> 00:03:57.997
Here is the IP header.

35
00:03:58.347 --> 00:04:05.515
Every datagram carries a time to live, the protocol of the layer above, a header checksum, and the source and destination addresses.

36
00:04:05.865 --> 00:04:09.919
Nothing is set up in advance. Each datagram is delivered on its own.

37
00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:15.197
IP makes no promise that a datagram arrives, arrives once, or arrives in order.

38
00:04:15.547 --> 00:04:17.659
That is the job of the layers above.

39
00:04:19.767 --> 00:04:23.073
When a datagram reaches a router, IP does four things.

40
00:04:23.423 --> 00:04:29.973
It decrements the time to live, and discards the datagram if it reaches zero, so a loop cannot circulate it forever.

41
00:04:30.323 --> 00:04:33.907
It calculates a new header checksum, because the header has changed.

42
00:04:34.257 --> 00:04:37.670
It finds the next hop, and obtains that hop's hardware address.

43
00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.881
And it forwards the datagram, one step closer to its destination.

44
00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:47.904
Networks carry different maximum frame sizes, called the M T U.

45
00:04:48.254 --> 00:04:54.249
Suppose a four thousand byte datagram must cross an Ethernet link with an M T U of fifteen hundred bytes.

46
00:04:54.599 --> 00:04:56.839
IP splits it into three fragments.

47
00:04:57.189 --> 00:05:02.437
Each fragment carries the same identification number, so the receiver knows they belong together.

48
00:05:02.787 --> 00:05:13.709
A more fragments flag marks every piece except the last, and the fragment offset, counted in units of eight bytes, says where each piece belongs: zero, one eighty five, and three seventy.

49
00:05:14.059 --> 00:05:16.619
Only the destination host reassembles them.

50
00:05:16.969 --> 00:05:22.644
Today, most hosts avoid fragmentation by discovering the smallest M T U on the path first.

51
00:05:24.767 --> 00:05:31.145
On a local network, frames are delivered by hardware address, not by IP address. ARP connects the two.

52
00:05:31.495 --> 00:05:40.071
A host that needs to reach 192.0.2.1 broadcasts a question to every host on the link: who has this address?

53
00:05:40.421 --> 00:05:42.789
The owner replies with its hardware address.

54
00:05:43.139 --> 00:05:49.006
And the answer is kept in the ARP cache, so the next datagram goes out at once, with no question asked.

55
00:05:51.133 --> 00:05:56.189
ICMP is IP's messenger. It reports problems and answers diagnostics.

56
00:05:56.539 --> 00:06:00.081
Echo request and echo reply are what the ping command uses.

57
00:06:00.431 --> 00:06:04.783
Destination unreachable reports that a network, host or port cannot be reached.

58
00:06:05.133 --> 00:06:11.341
Time exceeded is sent when a time to live runs out, and traceroute uses it to reveal every router along a path.

59
00:06:11.691 --> 00:06:14.357
Source quench once asked a sender to slow down.

60
00:06:14.707 --> 00:06:18.419
It was retired in twenty twelve, because it did more harm than good.

61
00:06:20.533 --> 00:06:25.632
At the transport layer, UDP, the User Datagram Protocol, is the lightweight choice.

62
00:06:25.982 --> 00:06:28.862
It is connectionless, and delivery is not guaranteed.

63
00:06:29.212 --> 00:06:33.969
Its header is only eight bytes: source port, destination port, length and checksum.

64
00:06:34.319 --> 00:06:40.079
That makes it ideal when speed matters more than perfection: DNS queries, voice, video and streaming.

65
00:06:42.200 --> 00:06:48.387
TCP, the Transmission Control Protocol, is connection oriented, and provides reliable delivery.

66
00:06:48.737 --> 00:06:52.427
Before any data flows, the two hosts perform a three way handshake.

67
00:06:52.777 --> 00:06:54.569
The client sends SYN.

68
00:06:54.919 --> 00:06:57.394
The server answers with SYN ACK.

69
00:06:57.744 --> 00:06:59.963
And the client confirms with ACK.

70
00:07:00.313 --> 00:07:07.054
From then on, every byte has a sequence number, the receiver acknowledges what it receives, and anything lost is sent again.

71
00:07:07.404 --> 00:07:11.948
Applications see a reliable, ordered byte stream, and never the packets underneath.

72
00:07:14.067 --> 00:07:20.595
With Windows NT 4.0, Microsoft built TCP/IP in as a core networking protocol.

73
00:07:20.945 --> 00:07:27.153
It offered universal compatibility: a standard, routable protocol supported across operating systems.

74
00:07:27.503 --> 00:07:31.919
Cross platform connectivity, with utilities such as FTP and Telnet.

75
00:07:32.269 --> 00:07:36.941
Scalability, through the Windows Sockets interface, for client server applications.

76
00:07:37.291 --> 00:07:43.776
And access to the Internet itself, connecting research institutions, government agencies and enterprises worldwide.

77
00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:52.065
No single organization owns or controls the Internet, but several oversee its protocols and standards.

78
00:07:52.415 --> 00:07:58.858
The Internet Society, founded in nineteen ninety two, promotes the development and adoption of Internet technologies.

79
00:07:59.208 --> 00:08:02.771
The Internet Architecture Board is its technical advisory group.

80
00:08:03.121 --> 00:08:07.217
The Internet Engineering Task Force develops and maintains the protocols.

81
00:08:07.567 --> 00:08:10.959
The Internet Research Task Force works on long term research.

82
00:08:11.309 --> 00:08:16.429
And the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority manages addresses and protocol identifiers.

83
00:08:18.533 --> 00:08:26.619
TCP/IP standards are published as Requests for Comments, developed by consensus. Each RFC keeps its number forever.

84
00:08:26.969 --> 00:08:34.649
A revision is published as a new RFC, the way RFC 791 replaced RFC 760.

85
00:08:34.999 --> 00:08:41.953
Requirement levels tell implementers what matters: required, recommended, elective, limited use, and not recommended.

86
00:08:42.303 --> 00:08:48.533
A specification matures in stages: proposed standard, draft standard, and finally Internet standard.

87
00:08:48.883 --> 00:08:54.493
Since twenty eleven, the process has used just two levels: proposed standard, and Internet standard.

88
00:08:56.600 --> 00:09:00.440
TCP/IP remains the cornerstone of modern networking.

89
00:09:00.790 --> 00:09:09.259
Four layers, a handful of core protocols, and one simple idea: move independent datagrams, and let the endpoints take care of reliability.

90
00:09:09.609 --> 00:09:16.415
Written down as a Department of Defense standard more than forty years ago, it still carries almost every connection on Earth.
